  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Added:\n- WriteDataAsync now accepts an IAsyncEnumerable<TEntity> alongside\n  IEnumerable<TEntity>. The sequence is enumerated once and lazily, so rows\n  produced asynchronously (by another database, an HTTP API or a queue) stream\n  straight into SqlBulkCopy instead of being collected into a list first. The\n  cancellation token is passed to GetAsyncEnumerator, so a producer written as\n  an asynchronous iterator observes it as well as the copy does.\n- DateTimeOffset, TimeSpan, DateOnly and TimeOnly can now be mapped, alongside\n  the DateTime that was already supported. A property of one of these types\n  used to throw an InvalidOperationException when the mapping was built, so a\n  table with a datetimeoffset or a time column could not be copied through this\n  library at all. Their nullable forms follow, as they do for every other value\n  type. Nothing is converted or reinterpreted on the way to SqlBulkCopy: the\n  offset of a DateTimeOffset reaches a datetimeoffset column intact rather than\n  being normalised to UTC.\n\nChanged:\n- SqlBulkOptions.EnableStreaming now defaults to false, matching\n  SqlBulkCopy.EnableStreaming. Nothing fails to compile and no copy changes\n  what it writes, so performance is the only place this shows. Turn it back\n  on for rows carrying a varchar(max), nvarchar(max) or varbinary(max) column\n  whose values are large enough that SQL Server stores them off-row, which is\n  the case the flag exists for:\n  WriteDataAsync(records, options => options.EnableStreaming = true). Where\n  every value fits in-row the previous default had nothing to stream and\n  still paid for the machinery, an allocation per column on every row.\n- WriteDataAsync no longer compiles when the compiler cannot tell which of the\n  two overloads is meant: an untyped null literal, and any argument whose\n  static type implements both IEnumerable<TEntity> and IAsyncEnumerable<TEntity>\n  (Entity Framework Core's DbSet<TEntity>, for example). Neither interface\n  converts to the other, so neither overload wins. Cast the argument to the one\n  you want, for example WriteDataAsync((IEnumerable<Record>)source), which also\n  states at the call site whether the source is read synchronously or\n  asynchronously.\n- The two remaining defaults are now documented rather than merely stated.\n  BulkCopyTimeout stays at 0, deliberately unlike SqlBulkCopy's thirty\n  seconds, because a copy of millions of rows outlives that limit and would\n  be abandoned part-written. CopyOptions stays at SqlBulkCopyOptions.Default,\n  which is what an unconfigured SqlBulkCopy uses.",
